# Without a file here, what git does to a text file on the way into the
# repository and on the way back out is a fact of each clone's core.autocrlf
# rather than of this tree, so two clones of the same commit can hold different
# bytes. That is untidy for source and wrong for evidence.
#
# Source and configuration are text. They are stored with a line feed and checked
# out the way the platform prefers, which is what a Windows editor and a Linux
# build each expect.
* text=auto

# Subtitle fixtures are bytes rather than text, and this is the line the rest of
# the file exists for. SubRip ends every line with a carriage return and a line
# feed, and a fixture exists to prove the exact bytes the writer produced. Under
# the rule above, a clone on Linux would check that fixture out with the carriage
# returns removed and a clone on Windows would put them back, so the file a test
# compares against would differ between two clones of one commit - and the test
# would go on passing on both, because it would be comparing the writer's output
# against a fixture that had moved with it.
*.srt -text
